(7434189, 1173759, 150277, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Congratulations on surviving your first entry into a challenge.  Unfortunately it does not appear that your image connected with many voters idea of a minimal scene.  I have to say I agree with some of the other commenters that the scene is quite busy, and for a minimalist scene I would expect a lot of negative space with one primary subject.  So your image to many of us just did not fit the challenge very well.  (Although there were a couple of people who gave you the top score of 10 too, so for some you really did connect quite well.)<br/> <br/>I would be interested in your comments on how this scene was minimal to you.  (I love when people describe their process, it helps me expand my thought process when I look at images so that I don''t dismiss a shot I think doesn''t meet the challenge.  Your kind of image can make me think harder about a topic, and what alternative ideas may be possible.)<br/> <br/>Technically, I like the image overall.  I love those weeds poking up and the lone man on the dock. I also like the drama and darkness in the sky. The tall post right by the boat I found a bit distracting since it pulled my eye away from the boat.  <br/> <br/>I hope you stick with us and show us more of what you can do.  It might not feel like you were off to a great start, but ignore the score and keep shooting.<br/> <br/>Julee<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. Congratulations on surviving your first entry into a challenge.  Unfortunately it does not appear that your image connected with many voters idea of a minimal scene.  I have to say I agree with some of the other commenters that the scene is quite busy, and for a minimalist scene I would expect a lot of negative space with one primary subject.  So your image to many of us just did not fit the challenge very well.  (Although there were a couple of people who gave you the top score of 10 too, so for some you really did connect quite well.) I would be interested in your comments on how this scene was minimal to you.  (I love when people describe their process, it helps me expand my thought process when I look at images so that I don''t dismiss a shot I think doesn''t meet the challenge.  Your kind of image can make me think harder about a topic, and what alternative ideas may be possible.) Technically, I like the image overall.  I love those weeds poking up and the lone man on the dock. I also like the drama and darkness in the sky. The tall post right by the boat I found a bit distracting since it pulled my eye away from the boat.   I hope you stick with us and show us more of what you can do.  It might not feel like you were off to a great start, but ignore the score and keep shooting. Julee ', '2016-02-02 12:03:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(7434246, 1173502, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Very nice shot, though possibly has too many elements for a truly Minimalist challenge.  Still, the light is great (don''t think you needed quite such a high ISO but oh well) and I know exactly how the water rose and fell, immersing the frozen drops of water off the branch each time, until they turned into these shapes. So capturing this when the water was still and mirrorlike is very good timing on your part.<br/> <br/>I have to disagree with the commenter who suggested that you should have cropped closer on the right.  However I would have suggested cropping out some of the upper left, where it''s starting to get a little overexposed, but it''s your image and how you choose to present it. I also think this would look great in b/w.<br/> <br/>Anyway, you did an excellent job and the commenters love you, so please keep entering and keep up the great work!<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Very nice shot, though possibly has too many elements for a truly Minimalist challenge.  Still, the light is great (don''t think you needed quite such a high ISO but oh well) and I know exactly how the water rose and fell, immersing the frozen drops of water off the branch each time, until they turned into these shapes. So capturing this when the water was still and mirrorlike is very good timing on your part. I have to disagree with the commenter who suggested that you should have cropped closer on the right.  However I would have suggested cropping out some of the upper left, where it''s starting to get a little overexposed, but it''s your image and how you choose to present it. I also think this would look great in b/w. Anyway, you did an excellent job and the commenters love you, so please keep entering and keep up the great work! Susan', '2016-02-02 17:31:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(7434250, 1173692, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Nice shot of mallard hen, and she has plenty of room to swim into. Ambient light is good and not harsh, ripples in the water and funky colours/patterns add interest.  But though technically a good shot, it''s just a little on the bland side.  This pic could have been taken anywhere, so I feel that is why it didn''t connect with viewers.<br/> <br/>One thing you may want to try in the future with wildlife: shoot at their eye level, not yours. Here you were clearly standing and shooting down at the duck.  Had you shot at her eye level, you may well have got a fantastic and different perspective and seen new things.  Never be afraid to change your point of view (aka pov).  Often it pays off.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps, keep shooting and entering!<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Nice shot of mallard hen, and she has plenty of room to swim into. Ambient light is good and not harsh, ripples in the water and funky colours/patterns add interest.  But though technically a good shot, it''s just a little on the bland side.  This pic could have been taken anywhere, so I feel that is why it didn''t connect with viewers. One thing you may want to try in the future with wildlife: shoot at their eye level, not yours. Here you were clearly standing and shooting down at the duck.  Had you shot at her eye level, you may well have got a fantastic and different perspective and seen new things.  Never be afraid to change your point of view (aka pov).  Often it pays off. Hope this helps, keep shooting and entering! Susan', '2016-02-02 17:38:11.000000', NULL, 0);
